course: world
lesson: 29
title: Classifying unusual animals
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: Some animals look as if they belong to one group but really belong to another, because we sort animals by their bodies, not by where they live or whether they fly or swim.
explain: A bat flies, but it has fur and feeds its babies milk, so it is a mammal; a whale lives in the sea, but it breathes air with lungs and feeds its calf milk, so it is a mammal too.
explain: A penguin cannot fly and swims like a fish, but it has feathers and lays eggs with hard shells, so it is a bird.
explain: A spider is not an insect: it has eight legs, two body parts and no antennae or wings; a woodlouse with fourteen legs is not an insect either, it is a relative of crabs.
explain: The platypus is a mammal with fur that feeds its young milk, yet it lays eggs, and a starfish and a jellyfish are not fish at all, because they have no backbone and no gills.
fact: Bats are the only mammals that can truly fly.
fact: Whales and dolphins are mammals: they breathe air with lungs and feed their young milk.
fact: A whale must come to the surface to breathe through its blowhole.
fact: Penguins are birds: they have feathers and lay hard-shelled eggs, but use their wings as flippers.
fact: Ostriches and emus are birds that cannot fly.
fact: Spiders are not insects: they have eight legs and two body parts; a ladybird, a small spotted beetle, is an insect with six legs.
fact: Woodlice are not insects; they are crustaceans, relatives of crabs, with fourteen legs.
fact: The platypus, which has fur and a flat beak like a duck's, and the echidna are mammals that lay eggs.
fact: Starfish and jellyfish are not fish, because they have no backbone.
fact: A seahorse is a fish: it has gills and fins and lives in water all its life.
fact: Snakes are reptiles, and some lizards, like the slow worm, have no legs.
fact: Turtles are reptiles that lay their eggs on land, even if they live in the sea.
exercise: Why is a bat a mammal and not a bird? | It has fur and feeds its babies milk | It flies only at night | It hangs upside down to sleep | It eats insects in the air
exercise: Why is a whale a mammal? | It breathes air and feeds its calf milk | It is the biggest animal in the sea | It eats tiny animals called krill | It can swim very deep in the ocean
exercise: How does a whale breathe? | With lungs, through a blowhole | With gills, just like any fish does | Through its skin while in the water | Through its mouth while under water
exercise: Why is a penguin a bird? | It has feathers and lays hard-shelled eggs | It walks on two legs on the ice | It lives in very cold places | It eats fish from the sea
exercise: What does a penguin use its wings for? | Swimming under water | Flying long distances | Digging nests in snow | Holding its food
exercise: Why is a spider not an insect? | It has eight legs and two body parts | It spins webs instead of nests | It eats other small animals | It is too small to be an insect
exercise: Which of these is an insect? | A ladybird | A spider | A woodlouse | A centipede
exercise: What is unusual about a platypus? | It is a mammal that lays eggs | It is a bird that cannot fly | It is a fish that breathes air | It is a reptile with feathers
exercise: Why is a starfish not a fish? | It has no backbone | It lives on the sea floor | It has five arms | It has no colour
exercise: Which group does a seahorse belong to? | Fish | Mammals | Reptiles | Insects
exercise: Which group does a sea turtle belong to? | Reptiles | Fish | Amphibians | Mammals
exercise: What is a slow worm? | A lizard without legs | A kind of earthworm | A kind of small snake | A kind of eel
exercise: Which bird cannot fly? | An ostrich | A barn swallow | A golden eagle | A town pigeon
exercise: Which group does a woodlouse belong to? | Crustaceans, like crabs | Insects, like beetles | Spiders, like tarantulas | Worms, like earthworms
exercise: What do bats, whales and people all share? | They are mammals that feed their young milk | They all live in the same habitat | They can all fly or swim well | They all lay eggs in nests
test: A new animal is found in a cave: it flies, has fur and feeds its young milk. What is it? | A mammal | A bird | An insect | A reptile
test: A sailor says dolphins are big fish. What is the best answer? | No, they breathe air and feed young milk, so they are mammals | Yes, they live in the sea and have fins | Yes, they swim and catch fish to eat | No, they are amphibians living in water
test: A small animal has six legs, wings and three body parts, but it lives in a pond. What is it? | An insect | A fish | An amphibian | A spider
test: A sea turtle comes onto the beach at night. What is it most likely doing? | Laying its eggs in the sand | Breathing with its lungs at last | Feeding its young with milk | Looking for leaves to eat
test: Which group includes both the penguin and the eagle? | Birds | Mammals | Fish | Reptiles
test: An animal lays eggs, has fur and a beak like a duck. Which group is it in? | Mammals | Birds | Reptiles | Amphibians
test: Which pair are both NOT insects? | A spider and a woodlouse | A bee and a garden spider | A beetle and a red wood ant | A fly and a grey woodlouse
test: Why must a whale come up to the surface again and again? | To breathe air with its lungs | To warm up in the bright sunshine | To catch the birds flying above the sea | To clean the sand out of its gills
test: A jellyfish lives in the sea, has no bones and no gills. Is it a fish? | No, fish have a backbone | Yes, it lives in the sea | Yes, it can swim | No, fish cannot sting
test: Which statement is true? | Some mammals can fly | All birds can fly | All sea animals are fish | All animals with eight legs are insects
test: is a whale a fish | No, it's a mammal that breathes air | Yes, it lives in water so it's a fish | Yes, it has gills like a fish | No, it's a reptile of the sea
test: can penguins fly | No, they use their wings as flippers | Yes, but only over the sea | Yes, they fly south for winter | No, they have no wings at all
test: my brother says a starfish is a fish cos it lives in the sea. true? | No, it has no backbone, so it's not a fish | Yes, all sea animals are fish | Yes, it has fins on its arms | No, it's a plant that lives in the sea
test: Leo says no mammal lays eggs. right? | No, the platypus and the echidna do | Yes, all mammals give birth to live young | Yes, only birds and reptiles lay eggs | No, bats lay eggs too
exam: Identify, from among the following, the only mammals capable of true flight. | Bats | Flying squirrels | Penguins | Ostriches
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises whales? | They are mammals that breathe air by means of lungs | They are fish that breathe by means of gills | They are reptiles that lay eggs at sea | They are amphibians that breathe through their skin
exam: Select the response that is consistent with the established facts concerning the platypus. | It is a mammal that lays eggs | It is a bird that cannot fly | It is a reptile with fur | It is a fish that breathes air
exam: Identify the principal reason for which a starfish is not classified as a fish. | It possesses no backbone | It possesses no colour | It is unable to swim | It dwells upon the seabed
